  "layouts": [
    {
      "id": "rwd",
      "title": "Rear-wheel drive",
      "description": "The engine sits lengthways at the front and drives the rear wheels. A long propeller shaft runs under the car to a differential on the rear axle.",
      "path": [
        "engine",
        "clutch",
        "gearbox",
        "propshaft",
        "differential",
        "driveshafts",
        "wheels"
      ],
      "notes": [
        "Steering and driving are split between the axles: the front wheels steer, the rear wheels push.",
        "On a car with a solid (live) rear axle, half-shafts inside the axle casing take the place of separate driveshafts."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fwd",
      "title": "Front-wheel drive (transverse)",
      "description": "The engine sits across the nose with the gearbox on one end. Gearbox and differential share one casing, the transaxle, and short driveshafts turn the front wheels.",
      "path": [
        "engine",
        "clutch",
        "transaxle",
        "driveshafts",
        "wheels"
      ],
      "notes": [
        "The most common layout for everyday cars: compact, light and no propeller shaft through the cabin floor.",
        "The front wheels both steer and drive, so the driveshafts need constant-velocity joints that work at an angle."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "awd",
      "title": "All-wheel drive",
      "description": "Every wheel is driven. After the gearbox a centre differential splits the drive between a propeller shaft to the rear axle and a second output to the front axle.",
      "path": [
        "engine",
        "clutch",
        "gearbox",
        "centre-differential",
        "propshaft",
        "differential",
        "driveshafts",
        "wheels"
      ],
      "notes": [
        "The path shown follows the drive to the rear wheels; the branch from the centre differential carries drive to the front differential and front wheels.",
        "Many all-wheel-drive cars replace the centre differential with a clutch pack that sends drive to the second axle only when needed."
      ]
    }
  ],
